-- =============================================================================
-- Multi-clause DECIDE groups are reported in terms of the clauses the drafter
-- wrote, not the CONSIDERs they compile to (review of legalese/l4-ide#545,
-- items 2 to 4). Warnings do not stop a file, so this one lives in ok/. This
-- tree's golden records only what each #EVAL gives; the warnings described
-- below are pinned in jl4-core/test/PatternClausesMissingSpec.hs.
-- =============================================================================
DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green, Blue
DECLARE Size IS ONE OF Small, Large
DECLARE Shape IS ONE OF
  Circle HAS colour IS A Colour
  Square

-- (2) A CONSIDER written inside a later clause's body still warns: no Blue.
GIVEN m IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `consider in clause two` (JUST n) c IS n
DECIDE `consider in clause two` NOTHING  c IS
  CONSIDER c
  WHEN Red   THEN 1
  WHEN Green THEN 2

-- (2) A definition whose name looks like one the compiler generates keeps
-- its warning: no Green, no Blue.
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
`__pm_fallthrough_0` c MEANS
  CONSIDER c
  WHEN Red THEN 1

-- (3) The first clause matches every input (its pattern is the input's own
-- name), so the two clauses after it are never used.
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `matches anything first` c     IS 0
DECIDE `matches anything first` Red   IS 1
DECIDE `matches anything first` Green IS 2

-- (3) A fresh name matches anything too, so the last clause is never used,
-- and neither is a clause that repeats an earlier one. Neither draws a
-- warning in this tree: that needs the redundant clauses of a coverage
-- analysis this tree does not have (the reference page says so).
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `fresh name in the middle` Red   IS 1
DECIDE `fresh name in the middle` other IS 0
DECIDE `fresh name in the middle` Green IS 5

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `repeated clause` Red   IS 1
DECIDE `repeated clause` Red   IS 2
DECIDE `repeated clause` Green IS 3
DECIDE `repeated clause` Blue  IS 4

-- (4) With no GIVEN to name the inputs, a missing clause is suggested with
-- `_` for an input the clauses leave open, never with a generated name.
DECIDE `no given` Red   Small IS 1
DECIDE `no given` Green Large IS 0

-- (4) A group of one clause is checked as a clause, and warned about at it.
GIVEN s IS A Shape
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `one clause` (Circle Red) IS 1

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `two clauses` Red   IS 1
DECIDE `two clauses` Green IS 2

#EVAL `consider in clause two` NOTHING Green -- 2
#EVAL `matches anything first` Red           -- 0: the first clause applies
#EVAL `fresh name in the middle` Green       -- 0, not 5
#EVAL `repeated clause` Red                  -- 1, not 2

-- (4) At run time, a group that runs out of clauses says so in clause terms ...
#EVAL `two clauses` Blue
#EVAL `one clause` Square

-- ... while a CONSIDER the drafter wrote keeps its own wording.
#EVAL `__pm_fallthrough_0` Blue
